<h1>Adding endpoints</h1>
<p>Register an endpoint so the controller can distribute test packages and execute steps on it.</p>

<h2 id="install-endpoint">Install the endpoint</h2>

<h3 id="install-from-pypi">Install from PyPI</h3>
<p>If the endpoint machine is connected to the internet, install it the same way as the controller:</p>
<table>
  <thead>
    <tr>
      <th>OS</th>
      <th>Command</th>
    </tr>
  </thead>
  <tbody>
    <tr>
      <td>Windows</td>
      <td>{{ cmd.copyCell("pip install orcraft") }}</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td>Linux, macOS</td>
      <td>{{ cmd.copyCell("pip3 install orcraft") }}</td>
    </tr>
  </tbody>
</table>

<h3 id="install-from-a-wheel-file">Install from a wheel file</h3>
<p>If the machine has no internet access, download the wheel on a connected machine, copy it over
  and install it directly:</p>
<table>
  <thead>
    <tr>
      <th>OS</th>
      <th>Command</th>
    </tr>
  </thead>
  <tbody>
    <tr>
      <td>Windows</td>
      <td>{{ cmd.copyCell("pip install orcraft-<version>-py3-none-any.whl") }}</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td>Linux, macOS</td>
      <td>{{ cmd.copyCell("pip3 install orcraft-<version>-py3-none-any.whl") }}</td>
    </tr>
  </tbody>
</table>

<h2 id="run-endpoint">Run the endpoint</h2>

<h3 id="insecure-endpoint-connection">Insecure endpoint connection</h3>
<p>For a fast setup on a <strong class="text-warning">trusted, isolated local network</strong>,
  the endpoint can be run without security enabled:</p>
{{ cmd.copyCommand("orcraft-endpoint run -secure disabled") }}
<p>This is how the endpoint's own terminal output looks like when running insecurely:</p>
{{ media.zoomableImage("/assets/images/screenshots/setup/endpoints/insecure-endpoint-run.png", "The endpoint's terminal output when started with security disabled", "large") }}

<p>In the controller, add the endpoint by clicking <strong class="text-accent">Add</strong> in the Endpoints frame.</p>
{{ media.zoomableImage("/assets/images/screenshots/setup/endpoints/add-endpoint.png", "Adding an endpoint in the Orcraft controller", "large") }}
<ol start="2">
  <li>Enter the endpoint's host name or IP and port, with security disabled.</li>
</ol>
{{ media.zoomableImage("/assets/images/screenshots/setup/endpoints/add-endpoint-dialog.png", "The Add Endpoint dialog, with security disabled", "large") }}
<ol start="3">
  <li>The endpoint appears in the Endpoints frame, connected without security.</li>
</ol>
{{ media.zoomableImage("/assets/images/screenshots/setup/endpoints/endpoint-added-insecure.png", "The endpoint added and connected without security in the Orcraft controller", "large") }}
<p>A <strong class="text-success">Ready</strong> status means the endpoint can now run test steps remotely.</p>
<p>Not interested in a secure connection right now? Skip ahead to the
  <a class="text-accent" href="/tutorial/">Tutorial</a> and come back to security setup later.</p>

<h3 id="secure-endpoint-connection">Secure endpoint connection</h3>
<p>For a secure deployment, follow the security setup procedure in the controller's Security tab:</p>
{{ media.zoomableImage("/assets/images/screenshots/setup/endpoints/security-tab.png", "The controller's Security tab, used to generate the authorization token and certificates", "large") }}
<p>Once that's done, run the endpoint with authentication enabled, which is the default:</p>
{{ cmd.copyCommand("orcraft-endpoint run") }}
<p>This is how the endpoint's own terminal output looks like when running securely:</p>
{{ media.zoomableImage("/assets/images/screenshots/setup/endpoints/secure-endpoint-run.png", "The endpoint's terminal output when started with security enabled", "large") }}
<p>If the endpoint was already added without security, right-click it in the controller and edit it
  to enable security instead of adding it again:</p>
{{ media.zoomableImage("/assets/images/screenshots/setup/endpoints/edit-endpoint.png", "Editing an existing endpoint in the Orcraft controller to enable security", "large") }}
<p>Otherwise, add the endpoint the same way as before, with the security option checked
  (enabled). Either way, it appears in the Endpoints frame, connected with security:</p>
{{ media.zoomableImage("/assets/images/screenshots/setup/endpoints/endpoint-added-secure.png", "The endpoint added and connected with security in the Orcraft controller", "large") }}
<p>A <strong class="text-success">Ready</strong> status means the endpoint can now run test steps remotely.</p>
<p>You can now proceed to the <a class="text-accent" href="/tutorial/">Tutorial</a>.</p>

<h2 id="auto-start">Start the endpoint automatically</h2>
<p>Optional, and a single command. Registering the endpoint for auto-start means it comes back by
  itself at your next logon, so a lab machine returns to service after a reboot without anyone
  having to remember to start it:</p>
{{ cmd.copyCommand("orcraft-endpoint startup") }}
<p>Any options you pass are baked into the registration, so the endpoint comes back exactly the way
  you run it by hand:</p>
{{ cmd.copyCommand("orcraft-endpoint startup -port 50052 -secure disabled") }}
<p>To check or undo it:</p>
{{ codeblock.block('orcraft-endpoint startup --status
orcraft-endpoint startup --remove', 'bash') }}
<p>Registration uses each platform's own user-level mechanism, so it never asks for administrator
  or root rights, and the endpoint starts when <em>you</em> log in:</p>
<ul>
  <li><strong>Windows</strong> - a Task Scheduler task. Nothing to install; Task Scheduler and
    PowerShell are part of Windows.</li>
  <li><strong>macOS</strong> - a launch agent in your own <code>~/Library/LaunchAgents</code> that
    opens the endpoint in Terminal.app. Nothing to install.</li>
  <li><strong>Linux</strong> - a systemd user service that opens the endpoint in a terminal window
    in your desktop session. A standard desktop install already has everything it needs.</li>
</ul>
<p>The one thing to know about Linux: because the endpoint is shown in a real terminal window,
  auto-start needs a graphical session and a terminal emulator on <code>PATH</code> - any of
  <code>gnome-terminal</code>, <code>xfce4-terminal</code>, <code>konsole</code>,
  <code>kitty</code>, <code>alacritty</code> or <code>xterm</code>. On a desktop image one of these
  is always present. On a minimal image, one package covers it:</p>
{{ cmd.copyCommand("sudo apt install xterm") }}
<p>On a headless Linux server with no desktop at all, skip auto-start and launch the endpoint the
  usual way - running it directly has no such requirement.</p>

<h2 id="troubleshooting">Troubleshooting</h2>

<h3 id="endpoint-status-online">Endpoint status: Online</h3>
<p>If the endpoint shows as <strong class="text-warning">Online</strong> instead of
  <strong class="text-success">Ready</strong>, the controller can ping it, but the controller and endpoint applications
  are failing to connect to each other:</p>
{{ media.zoomableImage("/assets/images/screenshots/setup/endpoints/endpoint-online.png", "An endpoint showing as Online instead of Ready in the Orcraft controller", "large") }}
<p>Possible causes:</p>
<ul>
  <li>A security mismatch: one side is running in secure mode and the other isn't.</li>
  <li>Both sides are in secure mode, but the security setup procedure in the controller's Security
    tab was not completed as required.</li>
  <li>A firewall is blocking the selected port on one of the machines - see
    <a class="text-accent" href="/setup/prerequisites/#tcp-port">Prerequisites/TCP port</a>.</li>
  <li>The port configured in the controller doesn't match the port the endpoint is listening on.</li>
  <li>The endpoint process was stopped or restarted after the ping succeeded.</li>
</ul>

<h3 id="endpoint-status-offline">Endpoint status: Offline</h3>
<p>If the endpoint shows as <strong>Offline</strong>, the controller cannot even ping it:</p>
{{ media.zoomableImage("/assets/images/screenshots/setup/endpoints/endpoint-offline.png", "An endpoint showing as Offline in the Orcraft controller", "large") }}
<p>Possible causes:</p>
<ul>
  <li>The endpoint machine is powered off, asleep, or disconnected from the network.</li>
  <li>The host name or IP address configured in the controller is wrong.</li>
  <li>The two machines are on different networks that cannot route to each other (for example,
    across an unconfigured VPN or subnet).</li>
  <li>A firewall or network policy is blocking ICMP/ping traffic between the machines - see
    <a class="text-accent" href="/setup/prerequisites/#icmp-ping">Prerequisites/ICMP (Ping)</a>.</li>
</ul>

